Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as new porous materials with enormous application potentials in gas adsorption,1 chemical separation,2 detection,3 catalysis,4 drug delivery,5 etc. Along maturing of MOF syntheses, it is now possible to design functional MOFs with various purposes and demands.6 On the way toward industrial applications, one major hamper is the high synthetic cost of MOFs. In addition to simply pursuing cheap MOFs, other design strategies to reduce the total industrial cost is awaiting exploration. In military area, a swing-role fighter can perform different roles by changing devices or software in a short notice, while a multi-role fighter aircraft carrying all kinds of devices can perform various roles in different missions. Both design strategies are well developed for cost-reduction, in contrast to the single-role fighter solely for a specific mission. Following such idea, cost-saving MOFs for variable applications may be achieved by developing swing-role or multi-role MOFs.
MTV-MOFs with multiple functional groups also have been developed to meet multipurpose. Yaghi and co-workers reported a series of MTV-MOFs containing up to 8 distinct functionalities in one phase.7 Cohen et al applied postsynthetic modifications to obtain multifunctional MOFs.8 By using linkers with specific geometry and different functional groups, Zhang et al could program the MOF pores for complex functional behavior.9 Choe et al developed stepwise pillar insertion route to connect 2D layers into multifunctional 3D-MOF.10 Recently, Zhou group developed a sequential linker installation strategy to precisely insert additional linkers for MTV-MOFs,11 and further engineered pore environment with up to three different functional groups.12 These excellent works suggest the possibility to create multi-role MOFs, even though uninstallation of multifunctional units was not achieved.
The key point to design swing-role or multi-role MOFs is to achieve facile functional switch, for example, through simple installation and uninstallation of different functional units (Figure 1). In a chemistry word, a reversible and dynamic chemical process is needed for such functional switch. Therefore, a swing- or multi-role MOF must have two vital features: 1) versatile functionalization with different modules; 2) easy transformation between different functional versions. If a basic framework can be functionalized and optimized for different purposes and reused after a simple function-change process without de novo synthesis, it could be applied for different missions in a swing- or multi-role way, thereof reaching the target of cost-reduction and high performance. This type of swing-role MOF strategy hasn’t been reported yet, although MTV-MOFs functionalized via irreversible linker installation11-12 and reversible incorporation of organic linkers into $\mathrm{MOFs}^{13}$ have been separately reported. Combination of these two important features in a single MOF is unprecedented.
A prototypical MOF competent for reversible functionalization and de-functionalization is found with LIFM-28 which was previously reported for demonstrating a post-synthetic variable-spacer installation (PVSI) strategy to predictably implement kinetic installation/uninstallation of additional spacers with different length and functionality.14 Only half of the replaceable binding sites are utilized to manipulate the breathing and adsorbing behaviors. Herein, we further explore the functionalization potential of this proto-Zr-MOF as a parent framework to achieve facile in situ multifunctional switch, i.e. a swing- or multi-role strategy for economic MOF applications. Taking advantage of all replaceable binding sites, a series of fully functionalized MOFs based on proto-LIFM-28 are generated, in which two types of additional spacers (shorter vs. longer as shown in Figure 2) are accurately inserted into two types of pockets, respectively. These fully functionalized MOFs, LIFM-70˗86 with isomorphous structures but variant functional units, can be obtained in one-step post-modification, and restored to parent LIFM-28. Such reversible and dynamic processes of spacer installation/uninstallation guarantee an easy multifunctional switch based on LIFM-28, and endow prototypical framework with various functionalities for targeted tasks via introduction of different functional units, e.g., gas separation, catalysis, click-reaction, luminescence, and extraordinary methane storage with a volumetric working capacity (Figure 2, vide infra).
The proto-LIFM-28 contains an 8-connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -cluster which has four pairs of replaceable $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ terminals as Site-A along $c$ -axis and Site-B along $a/b$ -axis. Two Site-As form a space named as Pocket-A suitable for insertion of shorter spacers of BPDC derivatives $(\mathrm{L}^{1-7})$ , while two Site-Bs build a space named as Pocket-B adequate for insertion of longer spacers derived from TPDC $\mathrm{{L^{8-}}}$ 13). All spacers carry distinctive functional units for orthogonal optimization of desired properties (See SI for synthetic details). Post-modification of proto-LIFM-28 can be readily accomplished by soaking pristine LIFM-28 crystals into DMF solution containing both short and long spacers under $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $40\mathrm{~h~}$ , offering functionalized versions of LIFM-70˗86 (Figure 2), respectively.
Single-crystal analyses of all functionalized MOFs (Table S1) confirm that the short and long spacers are pinpointed in PocketAs and -Bs, respectively (Figure S1), bringing in functional units including amine, trifluoromethyl, fluorine, methyl, phenolic hydroxyl, 2,2-bipyridine, Pd-coordinated 2,2-bipyridine and azide groups. The spacer installation changes the framework topology from 8-connected bcu to 12-connected bcu- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ (Figure 1), and consolidates the flexibility of proto-LIFM-28. Before installation, LIFM-28 has four types of straight channels (Channel-A and $^{-\mathrm{B}}$ along $a/b$ -axis, and Channel-C and -D along $c$ -axis, Figure 1). After installation, all A and B type channels are blocked. The biggest Channel-C is compartmentalized into two types of tetrahedral (Cage-A and -B), leading to narrowed pores $({\sim}5\mathrm{\AA})$ ascribing as cage-windows channels decorated with ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ and functional groups (Figure 1 and S2). Meanwhile, Channel-D $({\sim}9\mathrm{~\AA})$ is preserved with surface modified by functional units (Figure S2).
The pivotal feature of proto-LIFM-28 as parent MOF for multirole switch arises from its de-functionalization dynamics, simply accomplished via uninstallation of the inserted spacers. Similar to LIFM-29˗33 described in a PVSI strategy,14 LIFM-70˗86 can be recovered to LIFM-28 when soaked in water for sometimes (Figures 1a,b). This reversible process is representatively testified by crystal transformations between LIFM-28 and LIFM-77, 82, 83 and 86, and characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra (Figures 2, S3-6 & S63-66). The experiments started from a batch of LIFM-28 crystals to transform into LIFM-77 first, then turned back to LIFM-28. The second round is the interconversion between LIFM-28 and 83, and followed by reversible transformations to LIFM-82 and 86, respectively. The PXRD monitoring of these crystal transformations confirm perfect and repeatable structural interconversions, and further verified by the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption measurements which give the same adsorption
