capacity for the transformed and as-prepared LIFM-79 (Figure S89). These spacer installation/uninstallation experiments prove the parent LIFM-28 is robust enough to carry on successive and repeating functionalization switches, tunable between different functional versions and compatible with varied functional units. Hence, the proto-LIFM-28 is distinguished with two key features of a swing- or multi-role MOF, able to fulfill different tasks via repeated loading/release of diverse functional spacers.
The phase purity of all MOFs have been testified by PXRD patterns (Figures S7-24). $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectral examination of all digested LIFM-70˗86 in comparison with the starting LIFM-28 verifies nearly quantitative installation and uninstallation of one short and one longer spacers per $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ -cluster in LIFM-70˗86 (Figures S46-62 & Table S3), in agreement with the single-crystal results. To evaluate the stability and robustness for practical application, thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) and variable-temperaturedependent PXRD of LIFM-70˗86 are performed (Figures S27-45), revealing that most of functionalized MOFs can keep their framework integrity and crystallinity above $400^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ .
Nitrogen adsorption at $77~\mathrm{K}$ was measured for LIFM-70˗86 to evaluate their permanent porosity after activation (Figures S67- S85), revealing typical type-I isotherms of micropore nature. The BET surface areas range from 1079 to $1899\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , total pore volumes fall between 0.41 and $0.79\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and calculated pore sizes by DFT center within $10.9\mathrm{-}13.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ range (Table S4). In generally, the former two pore values are enlarged after spacer installation, except for those of bulky units; however, the comparable pore sizes of LIFM-70˗86 with LIFM-28 by DFT evaluation may only reflect the cage diameter, owing to compartmentalization of the biggest Channel-Cs with reduced window opening.
Since two types of spacers can be predictably and precisely installed to introduce variable functionalities and tune the pore attributes, an orthogonal optimization is readily applicable for the multi-role MOF to achieve optimal property desired as depicted in Figure 2. As carbon capture, methane storage and gas separation are main directions of MOF applications, we firstly demonstrate the swing-role strategy by optimizing the sorption properties towards different targets (Figures 3 and S86-104). Spacers ${\mathrm{L}}^{3}$ , $\mathrm{L}^{4}$ and $\mathrm{L}^{10}$ baring amine groups are installed into LIFM-28 to improve $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption owing to their well-known strong interactions with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules,18 which gives LIFM-77 and -79 with every unit cell containing 6 and 4 amine groups, respectively. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption analyses show an evident enhancement effect of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture upon amine functionalization, resulting in higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and isosteric heat $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ along with higher amine group density (Figures S86 & S98). The adsorption selectivities for binary mixtures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are calculated using the ideal adsorption solution theory (IAST),19 which offer exceptional $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation capability (Figures 4a & Table S5), being doubly or 6-fold increased to 9.9 and 34.7 by LIFM-77, respectively, in comparison with LIFM-28. Along this way, the optimal separation of chlorodifuoromethane (R22) — an ozone-depleting and potent greenhouse species — has been examined by introducing methylated or fluorinated spacers, giving LIFM-82 and -86. The results turn out a rather positive effect on R22 adsorption through functionalization with both methyl and trifluoromethyl/fluorine units (Figures S90 & S100). The initial IAST selectivities of $\mathsf{R}22/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ are increased by a factor of 2.6 with methylated LIFM-82 and 5.6 with fluorinated LIFM-86 compared with LIFM-28, respectively, reaching to 110.2 and 232.9 (Figure 4b &Table S6). These results clearly show that installing spacers with amine units successfully turn proto-LIFM-28 into functional version for carbon capture, while installing spacers with fluorinated units effectively change to another version for R22 separation.
Meanwhile, methane storage is an important MOF application, and methyl group is proved to benefit $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption,18 so LIFM28 is functionalized with different amount of $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ groups and transformed to LIFM-82 and -83 (Figure 3). Each unit cell of LIFM-82 and -83 has 6 and 4 methyl groups, respectively. Moreover, the installed spacers occupy open metal sites (OMSs) of proto-LIFM-28, which is helpful to working capacities since less $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is adsorbed at low pressure region.16 As expected, two MOFs show ultrahigh CH4 volumetric uptakes and working capacities (Table 1 & S7). At 80 bar, the total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes are $271\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for LIFM-82 and 265 for LIFM-83 (Figures 4c,d & S92-93), very close to the record of the best MOFs.15 Most strikingly, their volumetric working capacities at 5-80 bar and $298\mathrm{K}$ reach to 218 and $213\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. These values outperform the benchmark HKUST-1 under the same conditions, comparable with the record holder MOF-905 (noting that this compound was strictly evaluated by pycnometer density data considering defect
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
sites)15 and further verified by well repeatable adsorption measurements (Figure S94). Besides methyl group optimization and OMSs exclusion, the outstanding working capacity is also contributed by the reduced pore aperture and cage diameter15,18 owing to spacer installation to lead to compartmentalized cages.
For more general multi-role functional switches (Figure 2), LIFM-85 is designed to carry in an azide group, which opens a way for click reaction and successfully testified by transformation to LIFM-87 via cyclization with propargylamine (Figures S105- 108). Luminescence of proto-LIFM-28 is able to be turned on by inserting polyaromatic TPDC analogies (Figure S109), which is obviously responsive and tunable by different functional substituents. Furthermore, functionalization with catalytic-unit carrying spacers like $\mathrm{L}^{7}$ to switch to LIFM-81 type MOFs may offer a platform for catalysis study. These investigations towards multifunctional MOFs is underway in this group.
In summary, we describe a swing- or multi-role strategy for facile creation of MTV-MOFs based on dynamic post-synthesis with a robust $Z\mathrm{r-MOF}$ as parent framework. Through simple and pinpoint installation/uninstallation of functionalized spacers, pro$t o$ -LIFM-28 can be reversibly transformed to different functional versions for various application purposes. By virtue of orthogonal optimization, the amine functionalized LIFM-77 shows excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption and selectivity, the fluorinated LIFM-86 displays potential for fluorocarbons sequestration and separation, while the methylated versions of LIFM-82 & 83 show exceptional methane storage and working capacity at 5-80 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . Multifunctional switches to catalysis, organic reaction and luminescence are also promising. Therefore, the swing- or multi-role strategy may introduce a new avenue for MTV-MOFs toward multiply purposes via easy manipulating and total cost-reducing procedures.
